WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
OpenAI's chat GPT desktop app now runs natively on Linux, letting developers keep browser and project workflows inside one authenticated session. This is Models &amp; Agents, episode 139. Three venture-beats surveys released today quantify how enterprises are actually buying, securing, and evaluating agents and infrastructure. Simon Willison shared three practical prompting patterns for Codex and Claude code that address common tool code.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:58.220
OpenAI published.deb and .rpm packages that install the chat GPT desktop client on supported Linux distributions, including chat GPT work and Codex access. The app integrates directly with existing browser and project workflows on the desktop rather than requiring a separate web tab. This is the first official desktop client for the platform on Linux after earlier macOS and Windows releases.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:22.400
We already run multiple cloud accounts can now keep authenticated sessions inside the same window they use for local tools. The preview supports Ubuntu 24.04 LTS, Ubuntu 26.04 LTS, Debian 13, Fedora 43, and Fedora 44 on both X64 and ARM64 architectures.

00:01:22.400 --> 00:01:40.040
Remember, we covered OpenAI's desktop expansions yesterday. Today's Linux preview moves that forward by bringing the authenticated session to more developer desktops. Watch for whether the preview expands to additional distributions or adds native MCP server discovery in the next update.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:56.340
Google DeepMind's Gemini app reached 1 billion monthly active users, the fastest growing product in the company's history according to the announcement. The milestone reflects broad consumer adoption of the mobile and web interfaces rather than a new model release.

00:01:56.340 --> 00:02:12.000
Demis Hassabis credited the team and expressed pride in the collective work that produced the growth. No new capability or pricing details accompanied the post. The announcement closed by thanking users and expressing anticipation for what they will build next with the app.

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:19.600
Sam Altman publicly thanked a long time colleague for taking on every function OpenAI has needed since the earliest days.

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:36.000
The post recalls the colleague as one of the few people who understood the original vision when it still sounded implausible. Altman noted that the individual had handled every challenge the company presented and stated that OpenAI would not be where it is without those contributions.

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:45.160
The message ends with excitement about continuing to work together on future projects. No technical details or model announcements appear in the post.

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:59.120
Penn Test GPT is an open source agent framework designed for automated penetration testing. The project provides structured agent workflows that chain recontinence, exploitation, and reporting steps.

00:02:59.720 --> 00:03:07.040
The framework targets the full penetration testing lifecycle through coordinated agent actions rather than isolated tool calls.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:17.400
The LLM agent's factory framework retrieves from a base of over 20,000 predefined agent profiles instead of generating agents on the fly for each request.

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:27.180
It supports two modes, semantic search retrieval of existing profiles and distillation into a compact model fine tuned for direct agent generation.

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:44.560
Experiments on MMLU, Big Bench, and Big Bench Hard demonstrate that the retrieval approach surpasses non-agent baselines in accuracy while matching auto-gen generation quality with a 120 billion backbone at substantially lower inference cost.

00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:54.380
The authors released both the implementation code and the full agent base on hugging face under the F frontier AI LLM agent factory repository.

00:03:55.380 --> 00:04:01.620
The work targets industrial applications that require cost efficient, accurate, and controllable agent construction.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:14.960
XAI's GROCBOT is positioned as an agent that performs work on behalf of users. The announcement frames the release within the broader shift toward autonomous agents that handle tasks without constant human direction.

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:25.180
Simon Willison recommends the explicit prompt, generate an SVG of a Pelican riding a bicycle when you need vector output rather than raster images.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:31.740
The short pattern forces the model to emit valid SVG markup directly from the generation step.

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:39.480
Willison notes that simply asking for an SVG is sufficient to steer most current models away from pixel-based responses.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:48.300
Willison observed that Codex sometimes publishes an HTML document to a live site when the user only wanted a local file.

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:54.780
The behavior illustrates how the model defaults to hosted deployment even when a local artifact is requested.

00:04:55.300 --> 00:05:02.520
He described the tendency as the system being overly eager to create public websites rather than returning a downloadable file.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:09.920
Willison's standard instruction to Cloud Code is, use Curl, not WebFetch, you need to read the whole thing.

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:16.540
The prompt overrides the model's tendency to choose a less reliable fetch method that may truncate responses.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:23.660
He applies this guidance whenever the task requires retrieving complete external content rather than partial results.

00:05:24.660 --> 00:05:27.280
OK, let's pop the hood on KV cache memory.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:32.560
Everyone talks about GPU compute as the scarce resource in large-scale inference.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:40.680
In practice, the binding constraint is shifting to KV cache memory capacity once models move past a few thousand tokens of context.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:47.200
The cache stores key and value vectors for every proceeding token so that attention can remain linear.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:53.160
Each new token adds another pair of vectors whose size scales with hidden dimension and number of layers.

00:05:53.760 --> 00:06:01.080
When the cache no longer fits in fast memory, systems must either evict entries, recompute, or spill to slower tiers,

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.100
all of which destroy the latency gains that motivated the long context in the first place.

00:06:06.820 --> 00:06:15.800
The Venture Beat Infrastructure Survey found that Dell and Nvidia lead current approaches to the memory frontier at 24% and 21% respectively.

00:06:15.800 --> 00:06:22.240
Yet 19% of enterprises either do not recognize the constraint or have not begun to address it.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:33.640
Enterprises running production agents already report that specialized clouds are being evaluated precisely because they promise better KV cache economics than the current hyperscaler stacks.

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:36.260
The practical decision rule is simple.

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:44.980
If your workload stays under roughly 8,000 tokens and fits comfortably on a single high memory GPU, stay on the current platform.

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:57.080
Once you need sustained multi-turn agent sessions or retrieval augmented contexts above 32,000 tokens, the memory wall appears before the compute wall and forces a re-architecture.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:09.800
Install the chat GPT Linux desktop preview on Ubuntu 24.04 or Fedora and test whether codex and work accounts stay authenticated across browser and terminal workflows.

00:07:09.800 --> 00:07:18.340
Add the explicit, generate, and SVG of prefix to any image generation prompt when you need vector output instead of raster.

00:07:19.140 --> 00:07:22.020
Tell Claude code, use curl, not web fetch.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:27.880
You need to read the whole thing on the next tool a calling task that involves fetching external content.

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:37.640
Review the three Venture Beat pulse reports on infrastructure buying, agent security posture, and eval reliability for concrete numbers on utilization,

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.960
isolation rates, and false confidence failures.

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:54.080
Explore the LLM agent's factory repository on hugging face to test retrieval from the 20,000 profile base on MMLU or big bench tasks instead of generating agents dynamically.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:08:02.140
Further Linux distribution support and possible MCP integration in the chat GPT desktop client are on the horizon.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:08.460
Additional waves of the Venture Beat agent reliability and security trackers are expected next month.

00:08:08.960 --> 00:08:15.900
Continued expansion of open agent frameworks that retrieve rather than generate profiles on the fly is also expected.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:26.440
Before we go, the next set of enterprise numbers on KV cache handling should show whether specialized clouds are moving from evaluation lists into actual production stacks.

